  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this afternoon shows an expansive area of \nhigh pressure centered over Quebec, stretching west through the \nMiddle Mississippi Valley. The surface high will remain steadily \nanchored in place through the weekend as a developing \nnor'easter impacts the Mid-Atlantic and Northeast US. Here \nlocally, east- northeast winds have kept a continuous feed of \nlow stratus trapped beneath a subsidence inversion, holding \ntemperatures several degrees below normal. Aside from occasional\nbreaks in the clouds, mostly cloudy conditions will continue \nthe next few days with little change in the surface flow. \nBecause of this, temperatures will remain cooler through the \nweekend with highs peaking in the upper 60s to middle 70s.\n\nFurther west, an upper trough over the southwest US will lift \nnortheast, weakening as it approaches central US ridging. An \nassociated plume of tropical moisture ahead of this feature \ncurrently extends across the Plains states and will gradually shift \neast through the weekend, entering west-central IL by Friday \nmorning. Despite the lack of large scale forcing, occasional \nshortwaves in the upper flow will promote isolated to scattered \nlight rain showers west of I-55 through early Sunday. Little to no \naccumulation will result from these.\n\nBy early next week, broad ridging will take shape over the central \nUS as the nor'easter/surface low eventually moves into Newfoundland. \nA Pacific low will move onshore the coast of California Sunday \nnight/Monday and work into the central US by midweek. Forecast \nguidance differs on the strength and track of this system with the \nECMWF favoring a slower closed wave vs. the GFS's faster open \nwave. Despite the differences, a wetter and more active weather \npattern is favored by the middle to late parts of next week. \nAlthough the severe weather threat appears low at this time, the\npotential for heavy rain does exist.",
